---
/**
 * The open-source explainer.
 *
 * WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS RATHER THAN A BARE LINK. The header's "Fork this site"
 * action points here, not at GitHub. A repository landing page answers "what
 * is this" badly for someone who has not already decided they want it. It
 * opens on a file tree. This page gives the short answer first and keeps the
 * repository link where nobody can miss it.
 *
 * WRITTEN AT ROUGHLY A 3RD-GRADE READING LEVEL, ON PURPOSE, and that is a
 * tighter constraint than the articles, which sit around 9th grade. Short
 * sentences, common words, one idea each. If you edit this, read it out loud
 * first: anything you stumble over is too long.
 *
 * NO EM DASHES ANYWHERE IN THE COPY. Bryce does not use them. Where a pause is
 * needed the house move is a full stop, or an ellipsis, which is what the
 * articles actually do.
 *
 * CloseBot is disclosed in the prose, per AGENTS.md. An open-source page is
 * exactly where an undisclosed recommendation would read as promotion.
 *
 * THE PROMPT BLOCKS ARE MEANT TO BE PASTED VERBATIM. They are strings in the
 * frontmatter rather than text inside <pre>, because Astro preserves
 * everything inside a <pre> including this file's own indentation -- so markup
 * indented to match its neighbours would ship four leading spaces on every
 * line. Hand-wrapped near 60 characters so they do not scroll sideways on a
 * phone; `.prose pre` has no `pre-wrap` and DESIGN.md does not want new CSS
 * for this. If you edit one, keep the wrap. Do not reflow them into prose:
 * the whole point is that a reader copies them without reading them.
 */
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';

import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import chatWidget from '../assets/media/screenshot-2026-10-02-at-11-41-52-am.png';
import widgetConversions from '../assets/media/screenshot-2026-10-02-at-11-39-52-am.png';
import { person } from '../data/person';
import { SOURCE_REPO_URL } from '../data/site';

/*
 * The repository URL is interpolated rather than typed, per AGENTS.md: there is
 * one place this project's address is written and it is SOURCE_REPO_URL.
 */
const sayGetIt = `Clone ${SOURCE_REPO_URL}
into a new folder. Read README.md and AGENTS.md
before you change anything. Then install it, start
it up, and tell me what address to open.`;

const sayMakeItMine = `Open the "Forking this" table in README.md and do
every row for me. My name is ____. My email is
____. My links are ____. Delete the articles, the
photo, and the share cards. Those are not mine.
Then run pnpm bean && pnpm og && pnpm test, and fix
whatever fails.`;

const sayPushIt = `Make this a new repo on my GitHub and push it.
Call it ____.`;

const sayCloudflare = `Use my browser. Go to Cloudflare. Make a new Worker
from my GitHub repo. Use these settings:

  Build command      pnpm test
  Deploy command     npx wrangler deploy
  Root directory     /
  Production branch  main
  Build variable     PNPM_VERSION = 11.9.0

Give the Worker the same name as "name" in
wrangler.jsonc. Start the build and tell me when it
is done.`;

const sayRealAddress = `Put the workers.dev address Cloudflare just gave me
into SITE_URL in src/data/site.ts. Then push.`;

const sayPixel = `Use my browser again. Log me in to CloseBot. Go to
Source Settings, then Setup, then Sales Pixel. Copy
that id. Put it in my .env file as CLOSEBOT_SOURCE.
Then put it in Cloudflare too, as a build variable,
under BOTH tabs: Production and Previews Base.`;

const sayCheckPixel = `Now check my live page really has the chat script
on it. Do not trust the dashboard.`;

const saySafety = `Still in my browser, in Cloudflare under SSL/TLS:
turn on Always Use HTTPS, set Minimum TLS Version
to 1.2, and turn on HSTS and the No-Sniff header.
Then check from the command line that the header is
really being sent.`;

const sayDomain = `Add my domain to Cloudflare and point it at this
Worker. Then put it in SITE_URL and push.`;
---

<PageLayout
  seo={{
    title: 'Open Source: Fork This Site',
    description:
      'The code for this site is free. Fork it, and Claude Code puts it live on Cloudflare in about 30 minutes. The exact steps, and what to say.',
  }}
  heading="Open source"
  lede="The code for this site is free. Take it. Change the name. It's yours."
  width="wide"
>
  <Fragment slot="sidebar">
    {/*
      The repository link, in the slot the portrait fills on /about/. Someone
      who clicked "Fork this site" already knows what they want. They should
      not have to read anything to find it.

      `.kicker` and `.panel-shadow` are the named classes from global.css, not
      their Tailwind longhand. The longhand also had to re-declare
      `tracking-[0.08em]`, which `text-kicker` already supplies, and
      `transition-colors`, which the base layer already applies to every `a`
      at 120ms linear -- Tailwind's version eases and covers properties
      DESIGN.md's motion rule does not permit. `text-ink` overrides `.kicker`'s
      muted colour, the same way ThemeToggle does.
    */}
    <a
      href={SOURCE_REPO_URL}
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener"
      class="kicker panel-shadow inline-block border border-line px-4 py-2 text-ink no-underline hover:border-accent hover:text-accent-hover"
    >
      View on GitHub<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span>
    </a>

      <dl class="mt-8 space-y-4">
        <div>
          <dt class="kicker">License</dt>
          <dd class="text-small mt-1 text-ink">MIT. Free to use.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="kicker">Built with</dt>
          <dd class="text-small mt-1 text-ink">Astro and Cloudflare</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="kicker">Cost to run</dt>
          {/*
            Was "Just a domain". The steps below now reach a live site on a
            free workers.dev address with no domain at all, so that read as a
            cost the reader does not have to pay to get started.
          */}
          <dd class="text-small mt-1 text-ink">Free. A domain is extra.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="kicker">Time to live</dt>
          <dd class="text-small mt-1 text-ink">About 30 minutes</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
  </Fragment>

  <div class="prose">
        {/*
          {' '} before and after an inline link is deliberate. A newline there
          collapses to nothing and renders as "throughClaude Code". It is
          invisible in the source and obvious on the page.
        */}
        <p>
          This site is a test. I wanted to see if I could build a real website
          with AI doing most of the work. Not me typing code all day. An AI
          agent called{' '}
          <a href="https://claude.com/claude-code" rel="noopener">Claude Code</a>{' '}
          does the building. I tell it what I want.
        </p>

        <p>
          So far it works better than I thought it would. But there's a catch,
          and the catch is the whole point.
        </p>

        <h2>Why I made it free</h2>

        <p>
          Most SEO advice is just people talking. This is the real thing. The
          rules, the tests, and a live site you can go look at. You can check if
          it works instead of taking my word for it.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can also just take it. Want a fast personal site that's set up
          right? This one already is. Swap my name for yours and you're done.
        </p>

        <h2>How it works, in plain words</h2>

        <p>
          Every page gets built ahead of time. It turns into a plain file that
          sits there and waits. When you visit, the file is already done. No
          server has to think about it.
        </p>

        <p>
          That's why it loads fast. That's why it costs nothing to host. And
          that's why almost nothing can break.
        </p>

        <p>
          Here's the catch I mentioned. The site has rules, and the rules are
          not suggestions. A page can't go live without a title and a short
          description. Not "shouldn't." Can't. The site refuses to build. There
          are a few hundred checks like that. Broken links. Images with no
          description for screen readers. Pages that nothing links to.
        </p>

        <p>
          That matters a lot when AI writes the code. An AI will happily ship a
          page with no description, and nobody would notice for months. Here it
          fails right away and tells you why. The rules are what make the AI
          safe to trust.
        </p>

        <h2>Free chat box and visitor stats</h2>

        <p>
          {/* Disclosure in the prose, per AGENTS.md. Not inherited from the header. */}
          One more thing, and I should say up front that I co-founded the
          company behind it. So take it how you want.
        </p>

        <p>
          <a href={person.worksFor.url} rel="noopener">{person.worksFor.name}</a>{' '}
          gives out a free Sales Pixel. Drop that one code into the site and you
          get two things.
        </p>

        <p>
          The first is a chat box. It answers questions for people while you
          sleep, and it can take their email for your newsletter.
        </p>

        {/*
          EAGER, AND IT IS THE FIRST IMAGE ON THE PAGE. tests/seo/images.spec.ts
          treats a document's first <img> as its likely LCP element and fails a
          lazy one. This page is not an article, so it does not get the cover
          exemption that rule carves out.

          The heuristic is wrong here -- both images sit two thirds of the way
          down -- but the honest fix is to teach the rule about above-the-fold
          position, not to quietly exempt this page. Sized to the reading
          column rather than the 1282px original, so eager costs little.
        */}
        <Image
          src={chatWidget}
          alt="The CloseBot chat widget open on this site, showing an AI assistant greeting a visitor and answering a message"
          widths={[320, 480, 608]}
          sizes="(max-width: 38rem) 100vw, 38rem"
          loading="eager"
          fetchpriority="high"
          class="panel-shadow my-8 border border-line"
        />

        <p>
          You can change how it looks without logging in to CloseBot. The
          widget's styling lives in a file in this project, so an AI tool can
          edit it. I had Claude Code restyle mine to match the site. Square
          corners, my colors, no blue bubble.
        </p>

        <p>
          The second is numbers. You can see which pages people actually land
          on, and which ones get them to start a chat.
        </p>

        <Image
          src={widgetConversions}
          alt="A pie chart of chat conversions by page, with the home page the largest slice, followed by the writing index and the about page"
          widths={[320, 480, 608]}
          sizes="(max-width: 38rem) 100vw, 38rem"
          loading="lazy"
          class="panel-shadow my-8 border border-line"
        />

        <p>
          You don't have to use it. Leave it blank and there's just no chat box.
          Everything else works the same. But it's free, it's one line, and it
          covers two jobs most people pay for separately.
        </p>

        <h2>What's yours and what's mine</h2>

        <p>
          The code is MIT licensed. That's a fancy way of saying use it, change
          it, even sell what you make. Just keep the license file in there.
        </p>

        <p>
          My posts, my photo, and the share images with my name on them are not
          included. You'd swap those out anyway. The project lists exactly what
          to change. You get the engine, not my face.
        </p>

        <h2>How long this takes</h2>

        <p>
          About 30 minutes, and then it's live on the internet. Not a weekend.
        </p>

        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Step</th>
              <th>Time</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>On your computer</td>
              <td>5 minutes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Your name on it</td>
              <td>10 minutes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Live on the internet</td>
              <td>10 minutes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Chat box and stats</td>
              <td>5 minutes</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>
          Make two free accounts first, or the clock starts late. One at GitHub.
          One at Cloudflare. Both free, both quick.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't need a domain yet. Cloudflare hands you a free web address
          that ends in workers.dev. Your site goes there first. Point your own
          name at it later, once you have one.
        </p>

        <p>
          Here's the honest part. Setup is half an hour. The writing is not. The
          posts are the slow bit, and they stay the slow bit. No tool fixes
          that.
        </p>

        <h2>What to tell Claude Code</h2>

        <p>
          Six things to say. Copy one, paste it, read what comes back. Fill in
          the blanks with your own stuff.
        </p>

        <h3>1. Get it on your computer</h3>

        <p>
          You need Node 22 on your machine. Ask for it in the same breath if you
          don't have it.
        </p>

        <pre><code>{sayGetIt}</code></pre>

        <p>
          Look at what it reads first. The rulebook. That's the part that
          matters. It learns the rules before it writes a line, so it doesn't
          have to guess. Open the address it hands back and the site is there.
        </p>

        <h3>2. Put your name on it</h3>

        <p>
          The project keeps a list of every spot my name is hiding in. It's{' '}
          <a href={`${SOURCE_REPO_URL}#forking-this`} rel="noopener">
            a table in the setup guide</a>. Point Claude Code at it and let it
          work down the rows.
        </p>

        <pre><code>{sayMakeItMine}</code></pre>

        <p>
          My posts, my photo, and my share cards are not yours to keep, and this
          is the step that pulls them out. Don't skip it. Ship it with my name
          still in it and you've published a copy of me.
        </p>

        <h3>3. Put it on GitHub</h3>

        <p>
          Cloudflare builds straight from GitHub, so it has to live there first.
        </p>

        <pre><code>{sayPushIt}</code></pre>

        <p>
          It'll stop and ask you to log in to GitHub once. You do that bit. It
          can't type your password, and it shouldn't.
        </p>

        <h3>4. Put it on Cloudflare</h3>

        <p>
          This is the one people expect to be hard. Claude Code can drive your
          browser. You log in to Cloudflare, then hand it the wheel and it
          clicks through the setup for you.
        </p>

        <pre><code>{sayCloudflare}</code></pre>

        <p>
          Two things bite people here, so say them out loud. The Worker's name
          has to match the name in the project, or the build just fails. And
          that PNPM_VERSION line is not optional. Leave it out and the build
          dies with an error that never tells you why.
        </p>

        <p>
          Look at the build command, though. It's the test command. So the site
          can't go live broken. That's the catch from earlier, doing its job.
        </p>

        <p>When the build finishes you get a free address. Hand it back:</p>

        <pre><code>{sayRealAddress}</code></pre>

        <h3>5. Get the free chat box and stats</h3>

        <p>Same trick. Your browser, your login, its hands.</p>

        <pre><code>{sayPixel}</code></pre>

        <p>
          Both tabs. I mean it. They're two separate lists, and filling in one
          leaves your test pages with no chat box and nothing to tell you why.
          Nothing breaks. It's just quietly not there.
        </p>

        <p>So check the real page, not the dashboard.</p>

        <pre><code>{sayCheckPixel}</code></pre>

        <h3>6. Turn on the safety settings</h3>

        <p>
          Four switches live in Cloudflare and nowhere else. They can't sit in
          the code, because there's no server here to run them.
        </p>

        <pre><code>{saySafety}</code></pre>

        <p>
          The HSTS one has a trap in it. The box opens scrolled past its own on
          switch, so you can save it, watch it save, and have it do nothing at
          all. That's why the last line says check from the command line.
        </p>

        <h2>What it can't do for you</h2>

        <p>Three things, so none of them surprise you halfway through.</p>

        <p>
          It can't buy your domain. That needs your card. Buy one whenever you
          want it, then say this:
        </p>

        <pre><code>{sayDomain}</code></pre>

        <p>
          Call that ten more minutes, plus some waiting around for the rest of
          the internet to catch up.
        </p>

        <p>
          It can't type your passwords. You log in, every time, and then it
          takes over. Driving your browser needs the Claude extension for
          Chrome, and you hand it permission one site at a time.
        </p>

        <p>
          And it can't know what you want to say. It'll ask. That's the half of
          this no tool is going to do for you.
        </p>

        <p>
          Build something with it? I'd love to see it.{' '}
          <a href={`mailto:${person.email}`}>{person.email}</a>
        </p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
